@** The renderer. The renderer is the part of the program that talks to the
GPU. It owns the CUDA stream, the OptiX device context and pipeline, the
meshes and the acceleration structures built over them, the textures, and the
buffers into which the device programs write their pixels. The rest of the
program sees a small interface: hand it a [[Scene]], a camera and a few
switches, call [[render]], and read back an RGBA image together with the
identity of the mesh under every pixel.

Everything the GPU needs for one launch travels in a single structure,
[[LaunchParams]]; its definition is in \lpfile{LaunchParams.h}. The renderer
keeps a host copy of it, [[m_params]], updates its fields as the user changes things, and copies
it to the device right before every launch. Most setters below therefore do no
more than write one field.

@* The interface. The header declares the class [[OptixRenderer]]. It cannot
be copied: it owns GPU resources that must be released exactly once.

<<UsdOptixViewer/src/OptixRenderer.h>>=
// Ray traces a Scene with OptiX into a host-side RGBA image.
#pragma once

<<Headers and declarations of the renderer interface>>

class OptixRenderer
{
public:
    <<Renderer construction and initialization>>

    <<Renderer scene and view settings>>

    <<Renderer frame production and results>>

private:
    <<The device buffer helper>>

    <<The device buffers of one mesh>>

    <<Private steps of the renderer>>

    <<CUDA and OptiX handles of the renderer>>

    <<Scene and output buffers of the renderer>>

    <<Host-side results of the renderer>>
};
@ The header needs the CUDA types ([[CUdeviceptr]], [[CUstream]],
[[cudaMipmappedArray_t]]), the OptiX handle types, and the shared parameter
structure. The [[Scene]] is only passed by reference, so a forward
declaration is enough and the header does not drag in OpenUSD.

<<Headers and declarations of the renderer interface>>=
#include <array>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

#include <cuda.h>
#include <cuda_runtime.h>
#include <optix_types.h>

#include "LaunchParams.h"

class Scene;
@ Construction does nothing; the real work happens in [[init]], which may
fail (no GPU, an old driver, device programs that do not compile for this GPU) and reports the
reason as text instead of throwing, so that the application can show it in a
dialog and keep running without a viewport.

Normally the renderer runs on the first GPU. A headset's runtime may choose
another one, and the eye images can only be shared with it if OptiX renders
on that same GPU; [[init]] therefore accepts the GPU's UUID, a 16-byte
identifier that CUDA and Vulkan both report. [[release]] gives everything
back, so that the application can call [[init]] again for another GPU.

<<Renderer construction and initialization>>=
OptixRenderer() = default;
~OptixRenderer();
OptixRenderer(const OptixRenderer&) = delete;
OptixRenderer& operator=(const OptixRenderer&) = delete;

// Creates the CUDA/OptiX context and pipeline on the GPU with the given
// UUID (the first GPU if null). Returns false and sets `error` on failure.
bool init(std::string& error, const unsigned char* deviceUuid = nullptr);
// Frees all GPU resources; init() may then be called again.
void release();
const std::string& deviceName() const { return m_deviceName; }
const std::array<unsigned char, 16>& deviceUuid() const { return m_deviceUuid; }
@ The scene is replaced as a whole with [[setScene]], which uploads its meshes,
materials and textures and builds the acceleration structures over them. The view is described
by the image size, the camera and the light. There are two ways to set the
camera: [[setCamera]] takes the orbit camera's eye, target and field of view,
while [[setCameraView]] takes the more general description of a USD camera,
which may be orthographic and has a clipping range. The remaining switches map
one-to-one onto fields of the launch parameters and are read by the device
programs: shadows, mipmapped texture filtering, normal maps, traced
reflections, and the mesh to highlight as selected.

<<Renderer scene and view settings>>=
// Uploads the meshes, materials and textures and builds the acceleration structures.
void setScene(const Scene& scene);
void resize(unsigned int width, unsigned int height);
void setCamera(const float eye[3], const float target[3], const float up[3], float fovYDeg);
// halfWidth/halfHeight: the image plane at distance 1 (perspective) or half
// the view size in scene units (orthographic). Camera rays are clipped to
// [clipNear, clipFar] along `forward`.
void setCameraView(const float eye[3], const float forward[3], const float up[3], float halfWidth,
                   float halfHeight, bool orthographic, float clipNear, float clipFar);
void setLightDir(const float dir[3]);
void setShadows(bool on) { m_params.shadows = on ? 1u : 0u; }
void setMipmaps(bool on) { m_params.mipmaps = on ? 1u : 0u; }
void setNormalMaps(bool on) { m_params.normalMaps = on ? 1u : 0u; }
void setReflections(bool on) { m_params.reflections = on ? 1u : 0u; }
void setSelectedMesh(unsigned int meshId) { m_params.selectedMesh = meshId; }
@ Rendering is progressive. Each call of [[render]] traces one new sample per
pixel and adds it to an accumulation buffer on the GPU; the image handed back
is the running average. The counter [[frameIndex]] in the launch parameters is
the number of samples accumulated so far, so resetting the accumulation is
just setting it to zero. The caller must do that after any change that
invalidates the picture, such as a camera move.

The results are an array of RGBA pixels, ready to be uploaded to an SDL
texture, and a lookup [[meshIdAt]] used for picking with the mouse.

The eyes of a headset are rendered differently. [[renderView]] takes a
complete camera and writes into a buffer that already lives on the GPU (it is
shared with the XR runtime), and leaves the desktop image and its
accumulation alone. A head never stays still long enough to accumulate over
frames, so it takes a fixed number of samples per frame instead.

<<Renderer frame production and results>>=
// Adds one progressive sample per pixel and copies the average to the
// host buffers. Call resetAccumulation() after changing anything.
void render();
void resetAccumulation() { m_params.frameIndex = 0; }
unsigned int samples() const { return m_params.frameIndex; }

unsigned int width() const { return m_params.width; }
unsigned int height() const { return m_params.height; }
const std::vector<uchar4>& pixels() const { return m_pixels; }
unsigned int meshIdAt(unsigned int x, unsigned int y) const;
float lastRenderMs() const { return m_lastRenderMs; }

// Renders one view (an XR eye) into a device buffer of width x height
// pixels, averaging `samples` jittered samples. The desktop image and
// its accumulation are left alone.
void renderView(const Camera& camera, unsigned int width, unsigned int height, uchar4* target,
                unsigned int samples);
@ A [[DeviceBuffer]] is a block of GPU memory together with its size. OptiX
wants device addresses as [[CUdeviceptr]], a plain integer, so that is how we
store them. The helper is deliberately small: it can be resized, filled from
host memory, and freed. It has no destructor, because the order in which GPU
memory is released matters less than the fact that it is released before the
CUDA context goes away; the renderer's [[release]] frees every buffer
explicitly.

<<The device buffer helper>>=
struct DeviceBuffer
{
    CUdeviceptr ptr = 0;
    size_t size = 0;
    void resize(size_t bytes);
    void upload(const void* data, size_t bytes);
    void free();
};
@ Every mesh of the scene keeps its own vertex data on the GPU: positions,
shading normals and texture coordinates per vertex, three indices per
triangle, and the material of each triangle. Next to them lives the mesh's
geometry acceleration structure ([[gas]]) and the traversable handle by which
OptiX refers to it. Optional data that the mesh lacks, such as normals for a
flat-shaded mesh, is simply an empty buffer with a null pointer, and the
device programs test for that null.

<<The device buffers of one mesh>>=
// A mesh's vertex data and its GAS
struct MeshBuffers
{
    DeviceBuffer positions, normals, uvs, indices, triMaterial, gas;
    OptixTraversableHandle handle = 0;
    void free();
};
@ The private steps are the parts of initialization and scene loading that
deserve their own functions. [[buildAccel]] builds an acceleration structure
from one build input, and serves both the per-mesh structures and the one
over the instances; [[freeScene]] releases everything [[setScene]] created.

<<Private steps of the renderer>>=
void createPipeline();
void buildSbt();
OptixTraversableHandle buildAccel(const OptixBuildInput& input, DeviceBuffer& output);
void uploadTextures(const Scene& scene);
void freeTextures();
void freeScene();
@ The handles fall into three groups. First come CUDA and OptiX objects that
live as long as the renderer: the CUDA context and stream, the OptiX context,
the module compiled from the OptiX-IR, the program groups and the pipeline linked
from them, and the shader binding table with its three record buffers.

<<CUDA and OptiX handles of the renderer>>=
std::string m_deviceName;
std::array<unsigned char, 16> m_deviceUuid{};
CUcontext m_cuContext = nullptr;
CUstream m_stream = nullptr;
OptixDeviceContext m_context = nullptr;
OptixModule m_module = nullptr;
OptixPipeline m_pipeline = nullptr;
OptixProgramGroup m_raygenPG = nullptr, m_missPG[RAY_TYPE_COUNT] = {}, m_hitPG = nullptr;
OptixShaderBindingTable m_sbt = {};
DeviceBuffer m_raygenRecord, m_missRecords, m_hitRecords;
@ Then come the per-scene and per-image buffers. [[m_meshes]] holds the
buffers of every mesh, in the order of the scene's meshes, so that its index
is the mesh id. The device programs reach those buffers through
[[m_meshRecords]], an array of [[MeshData]] records with one device pointer
per array. [[m_instances]] holds the instances that place the meshes in the
world, [[m_ias]] the acceleration structure built over them, and
[[m_materials]] the materials of the whole scene. The image needs three buffers: the
8-bit colors shown on screen, the floating-point sums of the progressive
samples, and the mesh ids for picking. Finally, every texture is a mipmapped
CUDA array plus a texture object, and the objects are gathered in a device
table that the shaders index by number. [[renderView]] has its own scratch
buffers for the sums, the mesh ids and the parameters, so that an eye never
disturbs the desktop image.

<<Scene and output buffers of the renderer>>=
std::vector<MeshBuffers> m_meshes;
DeviceBuffer m_meshRecords, m_instances, m_ias, m_materials;
DeviceBuffer m_colorBuffer, m_accumBuffer, m_meshIdBuffer, m_paramsBuffer;
DeviceBuffer m_viewAccum, m_viewMeshIds, m_viewParams;  // scratch for renderView
std::vector<cudaMipmappedArray_t> m_textureArrays;
std::vector<cudaTextureObject_t> m_textureObjects;
DeviceBuffer m_textureTable;
LaunchParams m_params = {};
@
<<Host-side results of the renderer>>=
std::vector<uchar4> m_pixels;
std::vector<unsigned int> m_meshIds;
float m_lastRenderMs = 0.0f;
